sneak out a while, girls. No, thank you.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:20.890
Thank you, but no.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.900
Cold climate, Minnesota. Yes, isn't it?

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:33.410
Now, let's go down to the bar and talk it over.

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:33.990
All right.

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:36.720
I see you know our local hero.

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:37.790
We've met.

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:38.730
Charming fellow.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:40.330
Yes, he is.

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.010
I suppose you'll fall madly in love with him.

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:42.970
Everybody else does.

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:44.710
Except you, Mr. Ferris.

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:45.990
Oh, I think he's just ducky.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.400
Did you children enjoy your dance?

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:50.740
It was just ducky.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:52.780
Keith, you must sit down and have a drink with us.

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:53.280
I thank you.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:53.740
I'd like to.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:55.650
Hello, Anita.

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:56.250
Hello, hello.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:57.680
Dodo.

00:19:58.020 --> 00:19:58.560
Thank you.

00:19:58.740 --> 00:19:59.340
Good evening, Nathan.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:19:59.940
This is my niece.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.130
Helen Foster. This is Miss Ferris, dear. How do you do? How do you do? And Dr. Hartley.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:10.450
Why, yes, we met on the train. My name is Ferris, too. You, I know. A bulls, Major. Now, you two stay

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:20.670
right here and don't you move till we get back. The only dancer I know is a waltz. Will you risk

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:29.560
it with me? I'd love to, Dr. Hartley. And how are things at the separation center? Did you have a

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:29.940
good time?

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:31.370
Reno is delightful.

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:34.090
I received my discharge papers yesterday.

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:36.100
You'll get your copy in the mail.

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:38.300
Well, that calls for a celebration, doesn't it?

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:40.520
It certainly does.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:42.880
In that case, I'll take you down to the barn and buy you a drink.

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:44.140
Oh, really?

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:47.480
Oh, come now, we're civilized people, aren't we?

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:51.070
Well, I guess it can't do any harm.

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:53.770
You know, I think it ought to be a lot of fun,

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:56.030
now that we're not married anymore.

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:10.510
What do you think your chances are?

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:18.000
Ben, I built more hospitals in the South Pacific in one year than the average architect will see in a lifetime.

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:21.500
The only competition I'm worried about is Talbot and Talbot.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:27.980
Can't compete with them professionally, but they seem to know how to play the political side of it.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:29.280
Well, that won't count here.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:31.200
I hope not.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:34.070
Anyhow, I'm indebted to you for a crack at it.

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:38.160
I'm very suggestive that they invite a local architect to submit plans.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.570
Jeff, you're looking pretty seedy.

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:41.990
Sure you haven't been working too hard?

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:45.580
Well, it's been a pretty hectic week, Ben, but we've finished up tonight.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:47.800
Plans are all wrapped and ready for mailing in the morning.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:51.120
Hello, Ben, you old darling.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:52.000
Hello, Dodo.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:52.920
Hello, Jeff.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:55.200
There's an admirer of yours looking for you upstairs.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:56.020
Who's that?

00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:58.130
A girl by the name of Foster.

00:21:58.130 --> 00:21:59.770
You seem to have made quite a score.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:01.680
Aren't you speaking to me, Jeff?

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:03.030
Certainly, Jordan. How are you?

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:03.490
Fine.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.150
Are congratulations in order?

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:08.410
They certainly are. We're celebrating the new freedom.

00:22:09.610 --> 00:22:10.190
See you later, man.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:15.020
Bye. I think I'll go upstairs, Ben.

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.620
It's getting a little crowded down here.

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:37.880
Not too long.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:39.460
I'm completely exhausted.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:52.440
That song, do you know?

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.120
It's called Don't Forget Me.

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:57.380
Oh, I remember.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:58.800
They played it a lot nice to you.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:22:59.980
Yes, they played it a lot.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:09.120
it last fall is something the matter no i didn't eat my dinner i guess you shouldn't drink on an

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:24.310
empty stomach excuse me here but such a little animal and yet he's got an appetite as big as mine

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:29.980
well jeff was not sure well maybe

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:30.600
He left early.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:33.080
But he looked so worried last night.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:36.360
I suppose he's ill and there's no one to take care of him.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.820
After all, dear, Jeff's a big boy now.

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:41.010
Oh, how nice.

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:42.950
We're invited to Ben's fiesta next week.

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:44.410
He gives one every year.

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:47.600
I think I'll walk over and make sure he's all right.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:48.280
Who, Ben?

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:50.220
Oh, you mean Jeff.

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:42.390
Shattered.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:39.050
Did you see which way he went?

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:39.620
Who?

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:40.420
The man.

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:40.980
What man?

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:43.810
He was hiding in the bushes.

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:45.710
Didn't you see him?

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:46.440
No.

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:49.080
I only saw some branches move, that's all.

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:07.030
I've never seen lovely or rosy.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:11.200
What are they called?

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:13.970
Matador, I believe.

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:15.590
They grow in the bush out on the terrace.

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:16.510
Matador?

00:26:17.390 --> 00:26:19.810
Of course, after the color of the bullfighter's cake.

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:22.720
Bull's blood.

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:28.680
Jeff, I'm so sorry about Shamrock.

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:48.560
Do you understand?

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:51.470
Rose, I don't want to be safe.

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:54.860
I'll give you later.

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:57.000
All right, Jeff.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:55.910
Oh, thank you, Amelia.

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:57.320
Tell me, have you seen Major?

00:27:58.060 --> 00:27:59.240
Yes, she's right over there.

00:27:59.420 --> 00:27:59.780
Thank you.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:00.480
Please.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:03.160
Will you excuse me, please?

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:04.160
I bet.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:04.640
Yeah.

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.200
Major, I'll make yourself awake

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:10.120
from these charming people

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:11.980
and come and speak with Mrs. Atkinson.

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:13.640
She's dying to talk to you.

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:14.620
You know, for all the time.

00:28:15.140 --> 00:28:15.760
Go ahead.

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:19.250
Tell me, what does a certified public accountant do?

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:22.770
Especially a pretty certified public accountant.

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:25.400
I compile actuarial tables

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:27.220
for an accident insurance company.

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:29.900
What are the odds against a man fracturing

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.480
arm that sort of thing what are the odds 90 to one that you won't break your arm this year oh

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:41.820
that's good but if you do it's two to one then it'll be the right arm or have you noticed that

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:47.020
in your practice no what i've noticed is that my patients have all their fractures just when

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:59.890
i'm ready to sit down to dinner hey you oh si senor didn't i see you around my place last week

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:01.860
Me? Me, no, senor.

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:03.960
I'm sure I saw you hanging around my stable.

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:05.600
Me? No, senor.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:07.300
Some other fellow, but not me.

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:08.320
Just a minute. I...

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:15.320
That was all about it.

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:20.940
Oh, the morning after the dance, I went over to see if Jeff was all right and there was a prowler behind a bush.

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:22.190
Did you see him yourself?

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:24.840
No, but Jeff did.

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:28.500
Cigarette?

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:29.750
Thank you.

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:36.790
Did you ever compile any figures on the incidents of mental diseases for your insurance company?

00:29:37.330 --> 00:29:39.390
No, they weren't on our list.

00:29:41.440 --> 00:29:42.280
Interesting subject.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.040
Who's sane and who isn't?

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:45.840
Hard to tell sometimes.

00:29:47.610 --> 00:29:49.310
Do you know anything about paranoia?

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:52.820
It's a form of a hallucination, isn't it?

00:29:55.300 --> 00:29:55.780
Persecution.

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:58.930
It's with an inability to withstand stresses.

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:03.740
A normal person experiences misfortune, throws it off sooner or later.

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:09.290
If a person is psychotic to begin with, he may start to imagine things.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:13.620
He might even begin to imagine there's a conspiracy against him.

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:15.300
That's when it gets dangerous.

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.980
Do you have a specific case in mind, doctor, or person that you're treating?

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:21.050
No, it's out of my field.

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:23.290
I'm a general practitioner, not a psychiatrist.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.220
Senor doctor, lady called on telephone, very sick.

00:30:27.540 --> 00:30:28.380
You come right away?

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:29.670
Did you get the address?

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:31.100
Si, senor.

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:32.260
Oh, thank you.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:35.780
Please.

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:36.380
Of course.

00:30:41.980 --> 00:30:48.000
Are you sure, Jeff?

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:50.160
Ben, I'm positive it was the same man.

00:30:50.440 --> 00:30:53.600
Well, I'll talk to him if you want me to, but you know how it is with those fellas.

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:55.120
They all look alike if you don't know them.

00:30:56.860 --> 00:30:57.600
Maybe you're right, Ben.

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:04.910
The guy gets up and he says, Keith, you take a powder.

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:06.310
Oh, the guy was bigger than I.

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:07.350
What did I do?

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.110
Same thing happened to me, only he wasn't so tall.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:14.340
Oh, I remember all too well.

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:17.510
Excuse me, honey, I'll be back in a while.

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:18.060
Certainly.

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:22.440
The rest of the committee feel that a local architect should have the job.

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:24.410
Ben, may I see you a moment?

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:24.930
Surely.

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:25.810
Excuse me, Jeff.

00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:26.170
Certainly.

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:47.420
It isn't Miss Icicle from Minnesota.

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:49.140
Good evening, Mr. Farris.

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:49.900
Call me Keith.

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:52.620
It was nice of you to find this secluded spot.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:55.250
Are you always this way when you've been drinking?

00:31:55.390 --> 00:31:57.710
No, now, this Miss Prim and proper business didn't fool me for a minute.

00:31:57.810 --> 00:31:59.210
I spotted you the first night.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:31:59.850
Don't.

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:01.960
You don't have to pretend, honey.

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:03.480
Just relax and enjoy it.

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:03.980
Stop it.

00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:05.310
Are you kidding?

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:12.570
Jeff.

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:17.660
Galahad playing peeping Tom.

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:18.700
Get up.

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:20.000
Ridiculous.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:21.560
Would you mind leaving us alone?

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:22.370
Jeff, please don't.

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:23.690
I'd like to have a few words with him.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:24.920
Privately.

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:29.960
If I ever catch you around...

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.650
ellen again i'll run you on a pine cliff galahad you frighten me i'm warning you i mean it keep

00:32:35.650 --> 00:32:40.530
away from her i know too much about you so what you never open your mouth that's the beauty of it

00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:45.670
it's your story and you're stuck with it i don't have to open my mouth i can close yours what are

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:51.440
you two boys arguing about what does anybody ever argue about politics oh politics uh keith will you

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:55.990
do me a favor dr hartley had to leave on an emergency call will you see the daughter gets

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:59.970
home she has a little headache i'd be glad to then don't you think maybe i'd better

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.750
Look, I pay her alimony. I'll take her home.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:14.320
For the lovely, lovely party, darling.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.980
Glad you enjoyed it. You're sure I can trust you to take her straight home?

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:20.520
Oh, the car knows the way, Ben, all by itself.

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:21.560
Good night, then.

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:22.490
Oh, Ben.

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:22.990
Yes?

00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:25.530
It might be a good idea to keep an eye on Jeff tonight.

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:29.880
See that he doesn't drink anymore. He was getting a little, uh, a little out of control.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:31.580
Uh, thanks, Keith.

00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:32.690
I'll watch them.

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:33.190
Good night.

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:42.510
In the lab, I see.

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:43.930
Should have time.

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:45.340
Very little bit help.

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:46.290
Help?

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:49.410
Or is it just your meanness on your part?

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:50.560
Don't be stupid.

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:52.860
Ben hasn't any airs, has he?

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:55.920
I'm near not even a second cousin.

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:58.940
Suppose he should take a notion and leave it all to Calum.

00:33:59.020 --> 00:33:59.580
Where would I be?

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:02.170
Not in my ear the day after the will was filed.

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:04.680
Well, you don't think he's going to leave it to you, do you?

00:34:04.840 --> 00:34:07.640
No, I'm voting for charity.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:10.160
Keith Ferris is the administrator of the estate.

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:16.980
What would you think of a Vivian Shepherd Foundation firm?

00:34:17.180 --> 00:34:18.100
Or something or other.

00:34:18.740 --> 00:34:20.360
That turns even my stomach.

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:23.450
I'm very well, thank you.

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:26.280
You were in the cavalry, weren't you, Major?

00:34:27.630 --> 00:34:29.310
Well, I'd like to ask you a question.

00:34:30.160 --> 00:34:32.300
Can a horse break its leg in its own store?

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:35.100
What's that?

00:34:36.390 --> 00:34:37.890
You think it's a chance in a million?

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:43.940
No, I was just computing the odds on an accident that happened last week.

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:48.110
Well, thank you very much for the information, Major.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:49.960
Good night.

00:35:03.550 --> 00:35:03.850
Thank you.

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:00.560
Thank you.

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:01.390
the dog howling. I heard it too.

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:03.720
Could you tell where it came from?

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:07.890
No. It's around here someplace, but I can't

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:15.210
locate it. Listen.

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:35.070
Oh, Jeff.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:01.710
That's about all we can do tonight.

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:07.190
Jeff, I want to talk to you.

00:38:08.110 --> 00:38:08.670
Well, not now.

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:14.450
I didn't mean to be rude, but I'm just not in the mood for conversation.

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:16.390
You have to listen. It's important.

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:20.350
Go up to the house.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:44.140
Let's have it.

00:38:44.950 --> 00:38:46.370
I talked to Major Badger tonight.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:50.620
He can't understand how Shamrock could break his leg in his own store.

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:51.840
Well, he hasn't anything on me.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:53.020
I can't understand it either.

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:55.830
Unless it's luck.

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:57.130
Don't call it luck, Jeff.

00:38:58.280 --> 00:38:59.080
What would you call it?

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:07.430
I'm not sure. A few days ago, this rose was on your matador bush.

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:10.200
It was alive and beautiful. Now it's dead.

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:14.770
Was that bad luck or was it cause and effect? You picked it, so it died.

00:39:14.870 --> 00:39:17.430
I didn't pick it. The whole bush died overnight for no reason.

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:19.790
There must be a reason. There's always a reason for everything.

00:39:20.600 --> 00:39:29.130
Is there? Maybe you can give me a reason for this. Take a good look at it.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:35.220
looks different. Is it the way the light hits it? It isn't the light, it's the colors. Look at it

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:47.620
again. Oh, I see what you mean. They faded, haven't they? All the life's gone out of it. Your rose bush

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:57.400
died. Your painting faded. But have you ever had a common, ordinary fall, Jeff?

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:00.440
What do you mean?

00:40:00.710 --> 00:40:03.070
Sixty-five percent of all accidents are falls.

00:40:03.270 --> 00:40:07.770
Falls down a flight of steps, falls out of a tree, falls off a ladder, or in a bathtub.

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:10.160
But nothing of that sort ever happens to you.

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:11.000
It's too ordinary.

00:40:11.780 --> 00:40:13.480
Your kind of accident has to be unique.

00:40:13.990 --> 00:40:14.930
What are you driving at?

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:15.990
Your bad luck, Jeff.

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:17.490
It's a little too bad to be true.

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:22.070
Your porcelain figure fell off the table while nobody was in the house.

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:25.540
Shamrock broke his leg while he was in his own stall.

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:26.740
Now your picture fades.

00:40:27.460 --> 00:40:28.760
It's too bizarre, Jeff.

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:29.910
The impossible.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.040
can happen but not over and over again are you trying to tell me they didn't happen no they

00:40:34.040 --> 00:40:39.420
happened all right but it wasn't luck can't you see the pattern that runs through all your accident

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:46.080
shamrock mr o'grady your picture your statue your rose bush and the collision on the highway last

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:51.230
year that's when it all began six accidents and every one of them killed something you loved

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:56.080
you know what the mathematical odds are against a run like that a billion to one

00:40:56.080 --> 00:40:59.890
are you telling me they weren't accidents at all no

00:40:59.890 --> 00:40:59.910
Thank you.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.100
I'm only trying to tell you that lightning can strike twice in the same place, Jeff, but not six times.

00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:13.310
You mean you think that all of these things have been planned by someone?

00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:15.970
Someone who hates you, who's trying to destroy you.

00:41:16.330 --> 00:41:17.210
Who could it be, Jeff?

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:20.680
I don't know.

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:21.500
We'll find out.

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:24.720
We'll go back to the beginning, the night that Vivian was killed.

00:41:25.220 --> 00:41:27.300
We'll talk to the policeman who reported the accident.

00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:29.760
We'll find the garage they took the car to.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:33.200
We'll ask her questions about the accident and about Vivian.

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:33.500
Ellen.

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:34.600
We'll start tomorrow morning.

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:35.300
We'll look for witnesses.

00:41:35.560 --> 00:41:35.880
Ellen, wait a minute.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:37.560
Perhaps the ambulance driver will remember something.

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:38.820
I'll find him and I'll talk to him.

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:39.680
You won't talk to anyone.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:40.280
Why?

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:40.940
Why won't I?

00:41:40.960 --> 00:41:41.980
Because I don't want you to.

00:41:42.080 --> 00:41:42.520
But, Jack.

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:43.140
Listen to me.

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:45.880
I don't want you asking questions.

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:47.940
I don't want you investigating anything.

00:41:48.140 --> 00:41:48.960
This is my affair.

00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:50.380
I'll handle it my own way.

00:41:53.020 --> 00:41:54.360
I was only trying to help you.

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:58.180
Well, now you've let me know where I stand.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:41:59.640
You don't want any help from me.

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:02.040
All right, then, do it your own way.

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:04.040
Helen, listen to me.

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:06.120
Just don't let any black cats cross your path.

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:09.440
And if you see a horseshoe, pick it up and spit on it and throw it over your shoulder.

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:10.820
That ought to take care of everything.

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:12.050
Helen.

00:42:32.410 --> 00:42:41.250
Come in.

00:42:43.580 --> 00:42:44.380
Hello, Aunt Amelia.

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:45.800
You didn't tell me goodnight, dear?

00:42:45.980 --> 00:42:46.440
I'm sorry.

00:42:48.450 --> 00:42:49.170
Is anything wrong?

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:51.470
No, there isn't anything wrong.

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:54.710
There isn't anything right either.

00:42:57.390 --> 00:42:58.290
I saw you come home.

00:42:58.290 --> 00:42:59.870
You were at Jeff's again, weren't you?

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:06.310
yes i don't want you to think i'm an old busy body but i'm all the family that you have and

00:43:06.310 --> 00:43:12.630
it would help you to talk to someone what is there to talk about he doesn't love me he never will

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:20.710
he's in love with a memory i tried to warn you to be sensible sensible i love him

00:43:21.020 --> 00:43:24.120
i've got to help him if i can whether he likes it or not

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:30.610
Good night, dear.

00:43:31.650 --> 00:43:32.610
Good night, Aunt Emilia.

00:44:25.820 --> 00:44:27.440
I found out some very important information.

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:29.320
If you come over tonight, I'll tell it to you.

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:31.780
No, I can't tell you now.

00:44:32.150 --> 00:44:35.690
Look, I thought you understood I was going to handle this in my own way.

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:39.200
Don't argue, Jeff. Just come on over.

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:40.560
Goodbye.

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:47.000
Well, what do you think?

00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:49.790
It's hard to understand why it's faded.

00:44:50.050 --> 00:44:51.910
The artist didn't use pen colors.

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:53.010
He didn't even use temper.

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:54.280
There must be a reason.

00:44:56.150 --> 00:44:58.510
I would say this was faded with London watercolor.

00:44:59.030 --> 00:44:59.970
That's the best you can...

00:45:00.250 --> 00:45:06.960
Nothing fazes them except continuous exposure to strong sunlight for a little month.

00:45:07.060 --> 00:45:07.840
That's impossible.

00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:10.690
I can't imagine what caused it.

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:12.850
I'll tell you what I can do.

00:45:13.970 --> 00:45:15.850
I can talk to a couple of artists I know.

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:17.180
They might have an idea.

00:45:20.170 --> 00:45:22.740
I think my best bet is to go and see Trillfield.

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:23.780
He's a watercolorman.

00:45:23.920 --> 00:45:25.060
He's one of the best.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:27.260
He ought to know everything about it.

00:45:27.410 --> 00:45:29.130
I think I'll catch him tomorrow morning.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.300
You know Trilfield, the naturalist, he painted dogs, horses and cows.

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:36.700
My wife, he made a beautiful painting.

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:38.000
Thank you very much. I'm sorry it was trouble.

00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:39.660
Oh, no trouble at all.

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:41.040
Oh, Miss Foster.

00:45:41.260 --> 00:45:41.460
Yes?

00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:43.440
I just remembered something.

00:45:44.190 --> 00:45:48.410
Years ago, I framed some pictures for a painter by the name of Torres.

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:50.100
Vincente Torres.

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:54.080
One of the paintings was an old lady playing the guitar.

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:55.140
It was a water car.

00:45:55.220 --> 00:45:55.680
And his favorite?

00:45:56.880 --> 00:45:59.580
The man who bought the picture took sun lamp treatments.

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:05.780
winter and he took it on a couch right under the painting and he was mystified why the color faded.

00:46:06.340 --> 00:46:12.760
So Vincente found out that the rays from the sun lamp broke down the pigment. Can you remember the

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:19.900
name of the man who owned that picture? I don't think I ever knew because I'm a local man. What

00:46:19.900 --> 00:46:28.460
about the artist? Is he still in Pinecliffe? Torres, he died five years ago. Oh, thank you again.

00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:45.240
Very strange. Have you told anybody else about this?

00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:51.410
Of course not. Someone in Pinecliffe knows that you can fade the colors out of a painting by turning a sun lamp on it.

00:46:52.010 --> 00:46:55.970
If we can find out who owns that picture, Jeff, the portrait of the old woman with the guitar...

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:56.670
What would it prove?

00:46:57.720 --> 00:46:59.840
If somebody wants to destroy a painting...

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:23.760
be a hundred easier ways of doing it. It's not all. Read this. In the soil underneath

00:47:23.760 --> 00:47:28.720
the matador bush, I dug it out this morning. Enough arsenic to burn out the whole root

00:47:28.720 --> 00:47:29.120
system.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:31.740
It didn't get there by accident, Jeff.

00:47:32.120 --> 00:47:32.980
Someone put it there.

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:35.340
Someone who knows how to poison a rosebush.

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:37.200
Or a dog.

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:39.470
Or a man's life.

00:47:42.370 --> 00:47:43.470
Would you really like to help me?

00:47:43.550 --> 00:47:44.470
You know I would.

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:45.440
Then forget it.

00:47:46.270 --> 00:47:47.090
Forget the whole thing.

00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:49.170
But there was poison in the root system.

00:47:49.270 --> 00:47:50.050
The chemist told me.

00:47:50.050 --> 00:47:51.190
You never saw the chemist.

00:47:51.620 --> 00:47:53.840
You never heard of a portrait of an old woman with a guitar.

00:47:54.940 --> 00:47:55.600
Do you understand?

00:47:56.330 --> 00:47:56.710
No.

00:47:57.600 --> 00:47:58.860
No, I don't understand.

00:47:59.540 --> 00:47:59.820
Jeff!

00:48:00.700 --> 00:48:05.540
Jack! Your house! Look out the window! I saw it from upstairs!

00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:06.960
What? He's burning!

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:31.500
Everybody stand back.

00:48:31.940 --> 00:48:32.800
Out of the way.

00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:38.980
Hello, Jeff.

00:48:41.320 --> 00:48:42.500
Lovely fire, isn't it?

00:48:44.350 --> 00:48:45.590
Now you can see it for miles.

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:48.140
Jumped right in my car and drove over.

00:48:49.290 --> 00:48:50.490
Wouldn't have missed it for anything.

00:49:00.030 --> 00:49:00.970
So sorry, Jeff.

00:49:01.580 --> 00:49:02.960
Such a beautiful house.

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:05.960
I'm glad it's gone.

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:14.920
Be sure to tell Jeff that he's welcome to come to stay with us as long as he likes.

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:24.530
I did, but he left the house early this morning.

00:50:25.670 --> 00:50:27.170
Wait a second, here he is now.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:30.860
Robert wants to see you.

00:50:31.360 --> 00:50:31.940
So I hear.

00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:33.800
He'll be right in.

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:34.810
Thank you.

00:50:36.280 --> 00:50:36.860
He's here now, Stacey.

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:39.860
Didn't mean to keep you waiting.

00:50:40.250 --> 00:50:40.910
Quite all right, Jeff.

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:43.460
I'm terribly sorry about what happened last night.

00:50:43.580 --> 00:50:44.340
I know you are, thanks.

00:50:44.520 --> 00:50:48.060
I wouldn't bother you about anything today ordinarily, but Mr. Rogers just came to town.

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:50.720
This is Stacey Rogers, chairman of the hospital committee.

00:50:50.960 --> 00:50:51.340
Oh, of course.

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:52.060
How are you, sir?

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:52.560
I'm glad to know you.

00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:54.620
This is the Jeff Kohalan I recommended to you.

00:50:54.830 --> 00:50:55.190
Yes.

00:50:55.350 --> 00:50:57.650
We examined your plans last night, Mr. Kohalan.

00:50:58.130 --> 00:50:59.590
Good, clean, modern lines.

00:50:59.790 --> 00:50:59.970
Thank you.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:05.820
you. I'm sorry we couldn't give you the job. You couldn't? What? I was just about to tell you, Ben.

00:51:06.190 --> 00:51:13.640
We decided on Talbot and Talbot. You did? This is a surprise, Stacy. Which was it, Mr. Rogers?

00:51:14.510 --> 00:51:20.140
Politics or stupidity? I beg your pardon. If they'd given it to McCruder or Chesney and Pike,

00:51:20.260 --> 00:51:23.840
I wouldn't have said a word because I know the boys would have gotten a good modern hospital.

00:51:24.800 --> 00:51:29.320
But Talbot and Talbot haven't had a new idea since Burnham did the Flatiron Building. Oh, now take it

00:51:29.320 --> 00:51:29.560
Easy.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:35.720
not architects. They're political finaglers. Maybe you can tell me just one detail in their

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:40.750
plan that was an improvement over mine. Name just one. Young man, your attitude doesn't deserve a

00:51:40.750 --> 00:51:45.520
reply. But since you're a friend of Mr. Shepard's, I will say to you that no member of the board,

00:51:45.620 --> 00:51:50.680
myself included, could understand a reputable architect would submit his design for an

00:51:50.680 --> 00:51:57.780
important building with no interior detail whatsoever. No detail? There were 17 blueprints

00:51:57.780 --> 00:51:59.980
of the interior right down to the placement of the...

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:03.780
operating tables and surgery. You may have drawn them, young man, but you didn't send them to us.

00:52:05.430 --> 00:52:11.060
There were no interior details? None at all. Could they have been misplaced somewhere in your

00:52:11.060 --> 00:52:16.300
office? Not possibly. The sealed packages were open in the presence of the entire board. Uh,

00:52:16.670 --> 00:52:21.210
maybe you misplaced them, Jeff. You know how absent-minded you've been. Suppose you can find

00:52:21.210 --> 00:52:24.750
the duplicates. Would the board be willing to reconsider? That's impossible. The contract

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:25.750
has already been awarded.

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:35.050
This is quite a shock, Stacy. I felt sure that Coalham would get this job.

00:52:35.460 --> 00:52:37.780
I'm sorry, Ben, but you understand my position.

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:43.290
Jeff, you weren't going home without me, were you?

00:52:44.580 --> 00:52:45.020
Without you?

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:48.740
You told me to drop in at 5.30 and you'd drive me home. Do you remember?

00:52:49.570 --> 00:52:50.310
Going home now.

00:52:51.150 --> 00:52:51.910
Is anything wrong?

00:52:53.240 --> 00:52:56.090
Everything's wrong. Let's get out of here.

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:07.880
It's dreadful.

00:53:08.120 --> 00:53:10.780
A whole year's work completely wasted.

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:13.460
Typical Kohalan luck.

00:53:14.740 --> 00:53:15.580
Calling it luck.

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:20.090
Supposing someone took those plans out of the office and wrapped the package over again.

00:53:21.800 --> 00:53:23.760
Do a thing like that.

00:53:24.620 --> 00:53:25.160
Keith would.

00:53:25.470 --> 00:53:26.190
He hates you.

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:28.330
He hates everybody.

00:53:29.540 --> 00:53:29.960
Something twi-

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:30.920
Quisted inside of him.

00:53:31.480 --> 00:53:33.320
I would take a psychiatrist to explain it.

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:36.540
And why does Mr. Shepard keep him on at the office?

00:53:38.970 --> 00:53:39.890
He doesn't know what he's like.

00:53:40.530 --> 00:53:41.370
Joda must know.

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:43.920
She acts as though she's still in love with him.

00:53:45.010 --> 00:53:46.370
There are women who love men like Keith.

00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:49.460
Take a psychiatrist to explain that, too.

00:53:52.800 --> 00:53:53.540
It's getting kind of late.

00:53:53.600 --> 00:53:54.520
I'd better drive you home.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:13.260
Take me away.

00:54:13.460 --> 00:54:15.340
You say?

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:16.280
Nothing.

00:54:19.230 --> 00:54:20.350
Drive faster, will you, Jeff?

00:54:20.490 --> 00:54:21.890
I want to feel the wind in my face.

00:54:43.150 --> 00:54:45.680
296 Hall Street, Fall River, Massachusetts.

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:47.380
Thank you very much, Sergeant.

00:54:47.810 --> 00:54:48.870
Just one thing more.

00:54:49.480 --> 00:54:52.080
The accident happened on 12 Mile Drive, didn't it?

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:52.600
That's right.

00:54:52.740 --> 00:54:54.080
Just three miles from Pinecliffe.

00:54:54.620 --> 00:54:55.400
Thank you very much.

00:54:56.300 --> 00:54:57.660
Thank you, Department of Hospital.

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:01.930
Oh, Miss Foster, it's for you.

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:02.940
Me?

00:55:07.090 --> 00:55:10.160
Foster, this is Dr. Hartley.

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:15.890
My office is just across the street.

00:55:16.350 --> 00:55:19.410
I saw you go into the police station as I parked my car.

00:55:21.210 --> 00:55:23.090
Are you in trouble, Miss Foster?

00:55:23.480 --> 00:55:24.020
In trouble?

00:55:25.080 --> 00:55:25.720
Why, no.

00:55:26.510 --> 00:55:27.510
I mean, not exactly.

00:55:27.930 --> 00:55:29.610
I think you'd better drop into my office.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:32.160
Can you come over right away?

00:55:32.920 --> 00:55:35.820
Well, I have a long-distance phone call to make first.

00:55:36.530 --> 00:55:38.130
Well, just as soon as you complete your call.

00:55:38.700 --> 00:55:41.950
Yes. I think I can come by in about 20 minutes.

00:55:42.600 --> 00:55:42.940
Thank you.

00:55:48.700 --> 00:55:49.340
Thank you, Sergeant.

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:50.300
That's all right.

00:55:56.120 --> 00:55:57.160
Hello, Dr. Hartley.

00:55:57.360 --> 00:55:58.310
Come in. Come in.

00:56:00.030 --> 00:56:02.690
I was startled when you telephoned me at the police station.

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:05.670
Well, I'm very glad I spotted you when I did.

00:56:06.700 --> 00:56:08.600
Why did you ask me if I was in trouble?

00:56:10.230 --> 00:56:13.930
Well, the fact is, I'm in rather an awkward position.

00:56:15.670 --> 00:56:19.550
But your aunt is getting along in years, and I didn't want to disturb her unduly,

00:56:19.710 --> 00:56:21.830
so I thought it best to discuss this matter with you.

00:56:22.600 --> 00:56:23.420
Sit down, sit down.

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:24.300
Thank you.

00:56:26.530 --> 00:56:27.150
Yes, doctor?

00:56:28.300 --> 00:56:29.960
Since I'm your aunt's physician...

00:56:30.190 --> 00:56:34.890
I feel it's my duty to inform you that she isn't safe.

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:38.280
That neither one of you is safe, for that matter.

00:56:38.400 --> 00:56:45.370
So I wanted to get your permission to speak to your houseguest, to ask him to move to a hotel.

00:56:46.860 --> 00:56:50.760
But I don't understand. What is that to do with my aunt's safety?

00:56:51.420 --> 00:56:52.940
Yes, I was afraid you'd ask me that.

00:56:54.320 --> 00:56:56.560
The fact is, you're forcing me to take an unethical action.

00:56:57.840 --> 00:56:59.970
The man isn't my patient, and even if...

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:05.460
work whatever it is you're trying to say doctor why don't you just go ahead and say it yes you're

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:11.610
quite correct i'll come to the point let me say that i believe your house guest mr cohalan is

00:57:11.610 --> 00:57:17.270
definitely paranoiac he seems to have all the obvious symptoms of delusions of persecution

00:57:17.270 --> 00:57:24.590
if that's true then you and your aunt are in a hazardous position two helpless women in the

00:57:24.590 --> 00:57:28.320
same house for the man who may become dangerous at any moment we can't allow it

00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:32.170
May I ask you a question, doctor?

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:32.750
Certainly.

00:57:33.980 --> 00:57:40.870
You speak of delusions of persecution, but our delusion is imaginary, a thing that never happened.

00:57:41.320 --> 00:57:41.840
That's right.

00:57:42.560 --> 00:57:47.200
But Jeff's dog was poisoned. His house was burned down. That was no delusion. I saw it myself.

00:57:48.940 --> 00:57:50.760
Did you see the gasoline can?

00:57:51.880 --> 00:57:52.700
Gasoline can?

00:57:53.330 --> 00:57:58.640
The gasoline can the police found in the ruins of Hilltop. It was an unusual type of container.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:02.360
Only one store in Pinecliffe sells them.

00:58:03.240 --> 00:58:05.840
And that store sold such a can to Jeff last Monday.

00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:07.700
How do you know?

00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:10.120
The chief of police told me so this morning.

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:11.980
But I don't see how.

00:58:12.200 --> 00:58:13.100
And that isn't all.

00:58:14.550 --> 00:58:16.650
The body of the dog was disinterred.

00:58:18.190 --> 00:58:19.850
He died of arsenic poisoning.

00:58:21.400 --> 00:58:24.320
Two days after his master bought a package of weed out.

00:58:25.250 --> 00:58:28.210
Are you trying to tell me that Jeff burned down his own house?

00:58:28.330 --> 00:58:29.730
